{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And he shall present… et hakol, and he shall turn into smoke…</b> Behold, he already said this in the section on the cattle olah, and one cannot say that the intention is to include that which is not in cattle, namely the wool on the heads of sheep and the hair on the beard of he-goats, for all of these would not be inferred from his statement of ״et hakol״ in the cattle olah — for we find that they derived in Torat Kohanim the wool and the hair of he-goats from the verse stated regarding the cattle olah, and it is understood from them that the statement ״et hakol,״ even though it was written only regarding cattle, applies also to flocks in everything that pertains to them. Furthermore, here he did not say ״hakol״ except in connection with the presenting, and not adjacent to the burning, and our Rabbis of blessed memory (Torat Kohanim here) derived from his saying ״and he shall present et hakol״ etc. — this is the lifting of the lamb that requires a priest, a law that was not heard regarding the cattle olah. If so, why was burning on the altar necessary for a flock olah?" "<b>And it appears</b> to explain based on what they said in Torat Kohanim, and this is their language: ״And he shall turn into smoke״ — even if it went out, even if it is invalid, even if it is piggul and notar and so forth, when they are on top of the altar. End quote." "<b>And it is difficult:</b> from where did the Tanna find to derive the permissibility of invalid offerings on the altar? And the Rav, author of Korban Aharon, explained that since it did not write ״and he shall turn them into smoke״ [with a suffix], it implies that he may burn from any place. End quote. According to his words, his memory for blessing, one should likewise be required to derive from his saying ״and he shall present et hakol״ — from the fact that he did not say ״and he shall present them״ — that he may present from any place even if they are invalid; and if that is not so, then this too is not so. And the correct interpretation is that the Tanna derives it from the superfluity of ״and he shall turn into smoke״ ״and he shall turn into smoke״ — twice, and this is our very question: he should not have needed to say it except in one of the two places, either in the cattle olah or in the flock olah — but surely it comes for a derivation to include the burning of invalid offerings and notar and so forth after they have gone up onto the altar." "<b>An olah it is.</b> He said ״it is,״ and they derived in Torat Kohanim that this comes to require slaughter in the north after the fact, that if he did not slaughter in the north it is invalid. Therefore the Torah did not write the exclusion except in the section in which ״in the north״ is mentioned, to say that regarding this particular detail the exclusion comes, and it did not write it in the section on the cattle olah where semikhah is mentioned there — for on the contrary, regarding it comes the inclusion of the word ״olah״ (Leviticus 1:9), that semikhah mentioned there is not a requirement. And even though the verse states part of the inclusions and exclusions from one and part of the inclusions and exclusions in one, and we say as though he stated all the inclusions and exclusions in each of them, this particular detail is different, since it mentioned two commandments, which are semikhah and north, and it excluded and included, and it is not known for which of them it excluded and for which of them it included — we derive that which is adjacent to the exclusion and that which is adjacent to the inclusion: and what do we find? That which is adjacent to the inclusion is semikhah; that which is adjacent to the exclusion is north. And although there too an inclusion is stated, it comes to analyze dissection, that it is not a requirement, as inferred from the inclusion that was mentioned in the cattle olah. And there is yet further room to expand the aspects that exist in this matter, and the discerning one will understand on his own." "And in the baraita (Torat Kohanim here) a reason is given: they derived the inclusion regarding semikhah, that it is not a requirement, and the exclusion is regarding the north, which is a requirement, because semikhah does not apply to all olot, since a communal olah does not require semikhah and so forth. But perhaps they said this for the better outcome, for even though the matter was balanced, it could be resolved for the reason that we have written; and although the two sections are one matter, nevertheless to resolve such a doubt this would suffice. And you will find in the derash of ״an olah it is״ stated regarding the bird offering — our Rabbis of blessed memory derived in Torat Kohanim likewise: ״olah״ to include even if he did not squeeze out the blood of the head; ״it is״ to exclude if he did not squeeze out the blood of the body — end quote. And the Gemara (Zevachim 66b) raised the difficulty: what is the derivation? And the answer given is because most of the blood is in the body — end quote. See that by reasoning they inclined the doubt, and our precise reading from the verse is superior."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:52:43.473885118Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}